WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.500
The first medical emergency we'll discuss is heart attacks and the difference between a heart attack and cardiac arrest.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:12.500
In a heart attack, blood flow to part of the heart becomes blocked, which causes damage to the heart muscle.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:18.000
The person is usually still conscious and talking, though they are likely experiencing some of the symptoms shown here.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.500
It is also vital to understand that a heart attack doesn't look the same for everyone.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:29.000
While we often think of classic chest pain, women frequently experience much more subtle or atypical signs.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.000
This can include things like unusual fatigue, sudden nausea, lightheadedness,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.000
or even pain that is concentrated in the jaw, neck, or back, rather than the chest.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.500
Because these symptoms can feel less dramatic, women are sometimes less likely to seek help quickly.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.500
If you suspect something is wrong based on these signs, don't wait for classic chest pain to appear.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.500
Act on that suspicion and call 911.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:58.500
On the other hand, cardiac arrest is when the heart suddenly stops pumping altogether.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.500
The person instantly becomes unresponsive and stops breathing normally.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:10.500
That is your cue to start CPR and get an AED immediately because it is an immediate life-threatening emergency.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Now that we know what to look for, let's talk about what is actually happening inside the body during a heart attack

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.000
and, most importantly, exactly how you should respond.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Inside the chest, a clot is physically blocking the blood flow,

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.500
which means that specific part of the heart isn't getting any oxygen.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:33.500
If that blockage lasts too long, the heart muscle actually begins to die,

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.500
which is why we treat this as a race against the clock.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:41.500
The moment you suspect a heart attack, your very first move is to call 911

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.500
and have someone grab an AED to have on standby.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.500
While you wait for the professionals, help the person sit down on the ground

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.500
to reduce the workload on their heart and do your best to keep them calm and comfortable.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:59.000
If the person is alert and it is appropriate for them, you can have them chew and swallow one adult aspirin.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Keep in mind that a heart attack can often lead directly to cardiac arrest

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.000
if the heart damage causes the heart to stop pumping.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.000
This is why you must stay right there with them.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.000
If they ever become unresponsive or stop breathing normally,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.000
you need to be ready to transition immediately into CPR and turn on that AED.

